<section class="band shell">
 <dl class="acc" style="max-width:62rem" data-reveal>
 <details><summary>Availability logic</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>The rules determining whether a vehicle can be booked for a given period. Must account for turnaround buffer, maintenance blocks, <b class="kw2">one-way</b> rentals in transit and overbooking policy per category.</p></div></details><details><summary>Buffer time</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>A deliberate gap between one rental ending and the next beginning, allowing for cleaning, inspection and late returns. Omitting it is the most common cause of double-booking.</p></div></details><details><summary>Canonical tag</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>An HTML element telling search engines which URL is the authoritative version of a page. Pointing every page at the homepage effectively tells Google the rest are duplicates.</p></div></details><details><summary>Card hold</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>An authorisation reserving funds without capturing them, used for rental deposits. Distinct from a charge, and the distinction is where most generic booking plugins fail.</p></div></details><details><summary>Core Web Vitals</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>Google's page-experience metrics: Largest Contentful Paint, Interaction to Next Paint and Cumulative Layout Shift. They measure loading, responsiveness and visual stability.</p></div></details><details><summary>Doorway pages</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>Large sets of near-identical pages created to capture keyword variations. Explicitly against Google's spam policies. The distinction from a legitimate large site is whether each page carries genuinely unique substance.</p></div></details><details><summary>Fleet utilisation</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>The proportion of time vehicles are earning revenue rather than idle. The single most useful operational metric for a rental business.</p></div></details><details><summary>Geofence</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>A virtual boundary that triggers an alert when a vehicle enters or leaves it. Commonly used to detect unauthorised cross-border use.</p></div></details><details><summary>Headless commerce</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>An architecture separating the storefront from the commerce engine, connected by APIs. Adds flexibility and complexity in roughly equal measure.</p></div></details><details><summary>NAP consistency</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>Identical Name, Address and Phone across your website, <b class="kw2">Google Business Profile</b> and every directory listing. Inconsistency suppresses local search rankings.</p></div></details><details><summary>One-way rental</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>A hire collected at one branch and returned to another, which changes the vehicle's location and therefore its future availability. A frequent source of availability bugs.</p></div></details><details><summary>Rate card</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>A set of negotiated prices for a specific corporate account, applied instead of public pricing while running through the same reservation flow.</p></div></details><details><summary>Schema markup</summary><div class="acc__body"><p><b class="kw2">Structured data</b> in JSON-LD or microdata telling search engines what a page's content means rather than making them infer it. Prerequisite for most rich search results.</p></div></details><details><summary>Telematics</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>Vehicle data captured from an onboard device: location, mileage, fuel, engine diagnostics and driver behaviour. Now economically viable from around twenty vehicles.</p></div></details><details><summary>Utilisation rate</summary><div class="acc__body"><p>Revenue-earning days divided by available fleet days. Often confused with occupancy, which ignores vehicles out of service.</p></div></details>
 </dl>
</section>
